1. **A process for selectively hydrogenating a highly unsaturated hydrocarbon**, the process comprising:

   contacting a feed stream comprising at least one highly unsaturated hydrocarbon and hydrogen with a hydrogenation catalyst disposed in a reactor, under selective hydrogenation conditions effective to convert at least a portion of the highly unsaturated hydrocarbon to a corresponding less-unsaturated hydrocarbon;

   wherein the hydrogenation catalyst exhibits a selectivity of at least **90 mol%** to the corresponding less-unsaturated hydrocarbon, based on moles of the highly unsaturated hydrocarbon converted;

   wherein the feed stream further comprises carbon monoxide in an amount effective to moderate hydrogenation activity of the catalyst; and

   wherein the contacting is carried out at a **flow index (FI)** defined by the equation:

   \[
   FI=\frac{F \times C_{CO}}{V}
   \]

   in which **F** is a volumetric feed flow rate of the feed stream, **CCO** is a carbon monoxide concentration in the feed stream, and **V** is a catalyst-containing reactor volume,

   wherein the flow index is maintained within a predetermined range sufficient to provide said selectivity of at least 90 mol% while suppressing over-hydrogenation of the corresponding less-unsaturated hydrocarbon.
